Date: Tue, 17 Aug 93 22:02:47 -0400
From: pcm@cs.brown.edu (Peter C. McCluskey)
Subject: AI: slaves, selfishness, evolution

 price@price.demon.co.uk (Michael Clive Price) writes in #93-8-531:
>perspective.  The more intelligent an AI the more stable its primary
>goals will be, not less as our ego-centric viewpoints suggests.  Its

 Why?
 The more intelligent an entity is, the more phenomena it should be able to
alter. What do you expect to prevent it from altering its primary goals?
I am willing assume (although I am unconvinced) that you can program an
intelligence to want to avoid altering its primary goals, but I expect
that the intelligence would have to be either close to omniscient or
very restricted in its ability to handle uncertainty to avoid incidental
changes to its primary goals.
 There are lots of examples of existing intelligence producing solutions
to subgoals which have the effect of overriding the primary goals that
produced those subgoals (dying in order to reach paradise in heaven). I
submit that this problem is typical of intelligences of approximately
human power.

 To amplify on a point I made much earlier in this thread, I think much
of this disagreement may be based on differing assumptions about how
intelligence can be implemented. If it could be implemented with things
like a lisp statement (obey master), then your position would make some
sense. If, as I expect, it needs something more like a neural network
which is trained to maximize a function which approximates the concept
of "follow the intentions the master is trying to communicate via
command X", then the correctness of that network will only be known
for a subset of all possible inputs.

