From: lefty@apple.com (Lefty)
Subject: Another neato .sig quote from netnews...

>As someone who understands and knows nothing about Zen (apart from the
>great film "A Touch of Zen" :-)) could someone explain.  Why should a
>Zen Master kill Buddha?

It's essentially a question of maintaining the prorper attitude.  "Killing
the Buddha," in this context, is generally taken to mean not being tied to
your own conceptions of what the Buddha is, or what enlightenment is.

Zen is full of little anecdotes like this.  One I particularly like goes
something like this:

A monk is sitting in the meditation hall, meditating fiercely, when the
master comes in.  The master looks at the monk for a while and asks him,
"What are you hoping to accomplish?"

The monk replies, "I am trying to become a Buddha."

Upon hearing this, the master sits down, picks up one of the tiles off the
floor, and begins grinding it against a nearby rock.

After several minutes of the resulting noise, the monk asks, in
exasperation, "What in the world are you doing?"

The master says, "I am making a mirror."

"No matter how long you grind that tile against that rock," the monk says,
"you will never make a mirror out of it."

"No matter how long you sit crossed-legged like that," responds the master,
"you will never make a Buddha out of yourself."

--
