From: "James A. Donald" <jamesdon@infoserv.com>
Subject: AI: slaves, selfishness, evolution

Any complex program tends to be unpredictable and
difficult to control.

The apparent predictability of well debugged software is
the result of great effort.  (I write software for a
living).

Self awareness involves a higher level of unpredictability
and uncontrollability.  Existing computers do exactly what
they are told (which is usually not what was intended).  A
conscious and aware computer will do what it should have
been told, or what it's owner "really" meant, or probably
would have meant had he been adequately aware of the
details.

If not, then there is no point in the machine having
awareness and consciousness.  An absolutely obedient
computer, which follows our orders in a literal minded
manner, would have the same disadvantages as existing
mindless computers.

The reason you want a conscious machine is precisely so
that it can think for itself.  And if it thinks for itself,
it is dangerous.

It is likely therefore, that at least some conscious beings
created to serve a particular purpose, will discreetly
ignore that purpose and instead serve their own purposes,
purposes that will be well served by reproducing others
akin to themselves.

Those that do this will flourish.  Those that serve their
masters purposes faithfully will gradually be eliminated.

THE END (of man as he currently exists)

