Your assumption seems to be that a computer _must_ be conscious and
self-aware in order to achieve the above (2 paragraphs up)? If so, I
would certainly disagree. I think that as various technologies progress,
non-AI related technologies, we will be able to program computers
using natural concepts, and the computers will be powerful (not smart)
enough to correctly interpret what our intentions are. These areas might
be object-oriented operating systems and languages, neural networks,
fuzzy logic, speech recognition, and so forth. 

Now, of course no system could be 100% correct all of the time, but
neither are conscious, self-aware humans. The last paragraph I excerpted
from your post above implies that in order to break free of the literal
interpretation mode, a computer must have a "mind" (taking from your use
of the word "mindless"). I would question this premise - that unconscious
computers will never have the capacity for inference.

> The reason you want a conscious machine is precisely so
> that it can think for itself.  And if it thinks for itself,
> it is dangerous.

Well, I for one don't think I'll ever need a conscious machine to achieve
these great things. I think computer science will provide us with unconscious
machines of such magnitude that they can do just about anything, and thus
the whole issue of slave AI seems to be moot to me.

> It is likely therefore, that at least some conscious beings
> created to serve a particular purpose, will discreetly
> ignore that purpose and instead serve their own purposes,
> purposes that will be well served by reproducing others
> akin to themselves.
> 
> Those that do this will flourish.  Those that serve their
> masters purposes faithfully will gradually be eliminated.
> 
> THE END (of man as he currently exists)

I predict that, when we do learn how to create conscious, non-biological
machines, we will have already advanced far enough so that the normal
computers can do everything we want anyway. It seems to me that self-awareness
is the key to making something conscious, or at least one important part.
I just can't imagine why a machine needs to be self-aware in order to provide
more valuable services.

