Well, evolution seems to disagree with you.  I think it is highly unlikely
that consciousness emerged as an accidental by-product of natural
selection; rather, consciousness itself has survival value and was thus
selected for.

It seems likely that consciousness has biological costs, eg. our long
developmental time (both pre- and post-birth) compared to other animals;
the benefits must outweigh the costs or nature would not have made us
conscious.  (Hey, maybe "we" aren't, and I'm the only mutant who is!)

Of course I'm assuming a correspondence between our utility as gene-carriers
and the utility of machines to us.  This seems obvious, even to the point
of corresponding hazards of us making our genes obsolete (eg. uploading)
as machines making us obsolete.

